{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","statement":"The paper proposes a Dual Decomposition (DD) based algorithm that enforces agreement between English and Hindi parse trees as a constraint to disambiguate PP attachment.","source_span":"the formulation of the problem into a dual decomposition based algorithm that enforces agreement between the parse trees from two languages as a constraint"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","statement":"Experiments on the English-Hindi language pair improved attachment performance by 10% over the baseline.","source_span":"Experiments were performed on the English-Hindi language pair and the performance improved by 10% over the baseline"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","statement":"The baseline is the attachment predicted by the MSTParser model trained for English.","source_span":"the baseline is the attachment predicted by the MSTParser model trained for English"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","statement":"In the example sentence 'I washed the jeans with pockets,' the prepositional phrase 'with pockets' can ambiguously attach to either the verb 'washed' or the noun 'jeans'.","source_span":"consider the following sentence where the prepositional phrase with pockets may attach either to the verb washed or to the noun jeans"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","statement":"In the sentence 'I washed the jeans with soap,' the prepositional phrase 'with soap' correctly attaches to the verb 'washed' because soap is an aid to washing.","source_span":"the fact that soap is an aid to the verb washed disambiguates its attachment to the verb rather than the noun jeans"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","statement":"Two separate parser models, one for English and one for Hindi, are trained using MSTParser and used in the inference step.","source_span":"We train two separate parser models for English and Hindi each, using the MSTParser, and make use of these models in the inferencing step"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","statement":"Hindi case markers such as 'waali' and 'se' make PP-attachment clear, reducing ambiguity compared to English.","source_span":"The case markers waali and se in the two sentences in Hindi make the pp-atttachment clear"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","statement":"Ratnaparkhi (1994) used a Maximum Entropy Model to solve the PP-attachment decision.","source_span":"Ratnaparkhi:94 use a Maximum Entropy Model for solving the PP-attachment decision"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","statement":"Olteanu et al. (2005) treated PP-attachment as a classification problem using Support Vector Machines (SVMs) with syntactic and semantic features.","source_span":"Olteanu:05 have attempted to solve the pp-attachment problem as a classification problem of attachment either to the preceding verb or the noun, and have used Support Vector Machines (SVMs) that use complex syntactic and semantic features"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","fact_refs":[0,1,2],"question":"What algorithm does this paper propose for disambiguating PP attachment, and how much did it improve performance over the baseline on English-Hindi data?","answer":"The paper proposes a Dual Decomposition based algorithm that enforces agreement between English and Hindi parse trees as a constraint. On the English-Hindi language pair, it improved performance by 10% over the baseline, which was the attachment predicted by the MSTParser model trained for English."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","fact_refs":[3],"question":"In the example sentence 'I washed the jeans with pockets,' what is the source of attachment ambiguity for the prepositional phrase?","answer":"The prepositional phrase 'with pockets' could attach either to the verb 'washed' or to the noun 'jeans', making the sentence ambiguous."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Why does 'with soap' attach to the verb 'washed' rather than to 'jeans' in the sentence 'I washed the jeans with soap'?","answer":"Because soap is an aid to the act of washing, its semantics disambiguate the attachment toward the verb 'washed' rather than the noun 'jeans'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What tool is used to train the separate English and Hindi parser models in this approach?","answer":"Both the English and Hindi parser models are trained using MSTParser, and these models are used in the inference step of the algorithm."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Which Hindi case markers are given as examples that help disambiguate PP attachment?","answer":"The case markers 'waali' and 'se' are given as examples that make PP-attachment clear in Hindi."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What method did Ratnaparkhi (1994) use to solve the PP-attachment decision problem?","answer":"Ratnaparkhi (1994) used a Maximum Entropy Model to solve the PP-attachment decision."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0025","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How did Olteanu et al. (2005) approach the PP-attachment problem?","answer":"They treated it as a classification problem of attaching to either the preceding verb or the noun, using Support Vector Machines (SVMs) with complex syntactic and semantic features."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","statement":"The proposed dataset consists of 1900 collected dialogs.","source_span":"The proposed dataset, consisting of 1900 collected dialogs, allows simulation of an interactive gaining of denotations and questions explanations from users"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","statement":"The dataset enables testing the ability of dialog systems to learn new facts from user utterances throughout a dialog.","source_span":"This paper presents a dataset collected from natural dialogs which enables to test the ability of dialog systems to learn new facts from user utterances throughout the dialog"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","statement":"Current question answering approaches can only achieve around 50% accuracy on some question answering datasets.","source_span":"Current approaches BIBREF3 , BIBREF4 can only achieve around 50% accuracy on some question answering datasets"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","statement":"The paper identifies three sources of information for interactive learning: correct answers from users, broader explanations of questions, and rephrasing.","source_span":"The last source of information that we consider for interactive learning is rephrasing... First, the most straightforward way obviously is getting the correct answer for questions... Second, the system can ask the user for a broader explanation of a question"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","statement":"Freebase and Wikidata are given as examples of freely available knowledge bases containing large amounts of structured information.","source_span":"freely available knowledge bases, such as Freebase, Wikidata, etc. contain an enormous amount of structured information, and are still growing"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","statement":"The paper distinguishes three basic approaches to requesting knowledge bases, and notes a combination of these approaches is also possible.","source_span":"We distinguish three different basic approaches to requesting knowledge bases:\nA combination of the above approaches is also possible"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"How many dialogs are in the dataset presented in this paper, and what capability is it designed to test?","answer":"The dataset consists of 1900 collected dialogs, designed to test the ability of dialog systems to learn new facts from user utterances throughout a dialog."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What accuracy do current approaches achieve on some question answering datasets, according to the paper?","answer":"Current approaches can only achieve around 50% accuracy on some question answering datasets."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What three sources of information does the paper consider for interactive learning from dialogs?","answer":"The three sources are: getting the correct answer directly from users, asking users for a broader explanation of a question, and rephrasing."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What examples of freely available knowledge bases does the paper mention?","answer":"The paper mentions Freebase and Wikidata as examples of freely available knowledge bases containing large amounts of structured information."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0026","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How many basic approaches to requesting knowledge bases does the paper distinguish?","answer":"The paper distinguishes three basic approaches to requesting knowledge bases, and notes that a combination of these approaches is also possible."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","statement":"The newly collected crowd-sourced dataset used to evaluate the model covers four different domains.","source_span":"A newly collected crowd-sourced data set, covering four different domains, is used to demonstrate the effectiveness of the domain adaptation and open vocabulary techniques"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","statement":"The model architecture consists of a word embedding layer, followed by a bi-directional LSTM (bi-LSTM), and a softmax output layer.","source_span":"Our model has a word embedding layer, followed by a bi-directional LSTM (bi-LSTM), and a softmax output layer"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","statement":"In the multi-task model, only the output layer (slot embeddings) is individual to each app; the rest of the parameters are shared across tasks.","source_span":"In our study, only the relatively small output layer, which consists of slot embeddings, is individual to each app"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","statement":"The open vocabulary embeddings from Ling et al. use a character RNN to process a word one letter at a time.","source_span":"We address the generalizability issue by incorporating the open vocabulary embeddings from Ling et al. into our model BIBREF17 . These embeddings work by using a character RNN to process a word one letter at a time"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","statement":"The LSTM gates used in the model are as defined by Sak et al., including a linear projection layer on the output of the LSTM.","source_span":"The LSTM gates are used as defined by Sak et al. including the use of the linear projection layer on the output of the LSTM BIBREF22"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","statement":"As an example of shared morphemes, BBQ restaurants frequently use words like 'smokehouse', 'steakhouse', and 'roadhouse', while 'Bayside', 'Bayview', and 'Baywood' are all streets in San Francisco.","source_span":"BBQ restaurants frequently use words like \u201csmokehouse\u201d, \u201csteakhouse\u201d, and \u201croadhouse\u201d in their names and \u201cBayside\u201d,\u201cBayview\u201d, and \u201cBaywood\u201d are all streets in San Francisco"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How many domains does the newly collected crowd-sourced dataset cover in this paper?","answer":"The dataset covers four different domains."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the three main components of the model architecture described in this paper?","answer":"The model consists of a word embedding layer, followed by a bi-directional LSTM (bi-LSTM), and a softmax output layer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which part of the multi-task slot filling model is unique to each app rather than shared?","answer":"Only the relatively small output layer, consisting of slot embeddings, is individual to each app; all other parameters are shared across tasks."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How do the open vocabulary embeddings from Ling et al. work?","answer":"They use a character RNN that processes a word one letter at a time, allowing the model to share parameters between words that use the same morphemes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Whose LSTM gate definition does this model use, and what additional component does it include?","answer":"The model uses LSTM gates as defined by Sak et al., including a linear projection layer on the output of the LSTM."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0027","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What example does the paper give of restaurant names sharing morphemes that could help detect a slot?","answer":"The paper gives the example that BBQ restaurants frequently use words like 'smokehouse', 'steakhouse', and 'roadhouse' in their names, which share morphemes useful for detecting a restaurant name slot."}
